WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.040
What is multi-access edge computing? How is&nbsp;
it different than regular edge computing? Hi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:10.320
I'm Dan Kehn from IBM Cloud, and before I answer&nbsp;
those questions, please click like and subscribe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:17.360
Edge computing is about bringing compute capacity&nbsp;
closer where data is created to reduce response&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:24.640
time and the load on back-end servers. Multi-access&nbsp;
edge computing is the next logical step in edge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:30.560
computing for telco cloud. It brings compute&nbsp;
capacity directly to network's edge, literally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:37.440
on the same infrastructure as the network itself.&nbsp;
Let's go through it using an example. So we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:47.360
say an image application that does image&nbsp;
analysis. It communicates with the core network.

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:57.840
That in turn works with a back-end server.

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:06.560
For sake of discussion, on that&nbsp;
back-end server, let's assume we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:11.840
a service called "image analysis service" installed.

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:18.960
Okay this is a classical&nbsp;
application design pattern;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:21.840
the user makes a request, it&nbsp;
goes to a back-end server,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:27.360
it formulates a response that it returns the end&nbsp;
user. The round trip for that including latency&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:32.560
might be on the order of 100 milliseconds.&nbsp;
That's perfectly fine for many applications.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:38.000
But let's assume we have a more demanding&nbsp;
application, for example, one that does video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:46.320
and it isn't using just one user that it wants&nbsp;
to capture, it's capturing a crowd. Maybe it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:50.560
used for security purposes, maybe it's used for&nbsp;
threat analysis, there's just some examples.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:58.720
What really drives us towards an edge computing&nbsp;
solution are two things: First, there's a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:03.760
of data. Now an HD video camera, that could&nbsp;
stream as much as six megabits per second.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:09.440
The other thing is the real-time&nbsp;
aspect. If we need a real-time response,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:15.680
it would be difficult to send all that data&nbsp;
back to the back-end server, process it, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:22.400
return it in real-time. That's why we install edge&nbsp;
servers closer to where the source of data is.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:26.640
So say, for example, we install some edge servers,

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:35.840
and on those we install&nbsp;
the "video analysis service".

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:43.760
This really helped with our latency problem. We&nbsp;
no longer have to send data across the network&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:49.840
to a far away server. It also&nbsp;
helps with our data volume problem.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:54.320
We're no longer sending huge&nbsp;
amounts of data across the network.

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:03.840
Where multi-access edge computing comes&nbsp;
into play is when we add a third element,

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:09.520
specifically mobility.

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:18.640
In the prior examples, we were assuming that&nbsp;
these edge computers were in a fixed location,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:24.400
for example, at a retail location providing a&nbsp;
shopping experience, maybe an IoT device at a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:30.160
factory that's doing assembly. Those are examples&nbsp;
where the edge computer location is known.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:37.360
In this case for multi-access edge computing, it&nbsp;
could be anywhere. We cannot predict where the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:43.120
edge computers might be located, so we install&nbsp;
them on the network itself. So, for example, we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:53.920
have a radio access network and then install&nbsp;
it at the base station; or, we could install it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:59.840
at the data center for the network itself. This&nbsp;
really drives latency to its absolute minimum,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:06.880
as little as 10 milliseconds. It also reduces the&nbsp;
load on the back-end server for the enterprise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:11.200
We could then repurpose that for something&nbsp;
else, for example, we could do machine learning.

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:18.640
So, for example, say we do machine learning&nbsp;
training, then we take that result and we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:23.040
give it to the video analysis service&nbsp;
to improve the quality of its results.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:28.240
There's a couple other different benefits&nbsp;
that are worth noting. Because this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:33.360
running on the network's infrastructure,&nbsp;
if there is a temporary outage between the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:41.840
back-end server and video analysis, it can continue&nbsp;
to process. Thus you have continuous operations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:47.120
Finally, there is the ability to take&nbsp;
advantage of radio access network data.

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:57.680
For example, the radio access network would&nbsp;
know where the location of the users are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:03.920
and it could potentially predict where additional&nbsp;
load would be required. This allows their network&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:07.841
to be much more responsive to users' needs and&nbsp;
being able to deploy capacity where it's going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.841 --> 00:05:10.243
where it's going to&nbsp;be needed in the future.

00:05:10.443 --> 00:05:14.640
Multi-access&nbsp;edge computing means communication service&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:20.000
providers can bring compute capacity directly to&nbsp;
your users no matter where or how they connect to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:25.200
the network. Thank you for watching. If you&nbsp;
have questions please drop us a line below.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:29.600
If you want to see more videos like this&nbsp;
in the future, please like and subscribe.

